## **6.3 Relay coil power**

The relay coil needs 12 V.

The external supply is 20 V, so we need a small buck converter:

\\[ 20\text{ V}\rightarrow12\text{ V} \\]

The relay coil connects across this converter's 12 V output.

It must be connected in parallel, not in series with the main power supply.

If the relay does not have built-in coil suppression, a flyback diode can be installed across the coil:

- Diode cathode to +12 V.
- Diode anode to ground.

If the relay contains an internal diode, its marked polarity must be followed.

The coil supply must only energize the relay when the external PD supply is available.

## **6.4 Main buck-boost converter**

This is the second regulator.

The battery voltage changes during use:

- Fully charged: 12.6 V.
- Nominal: 11.1 V.
- Near the lower operating region: approximately 9 V, subject to the cell specifications and cutoff settings.

The external PD supply is 20 V.

The cooler requires a regulated 12 V supply.

Therefore, the selected converter must accept the complete input range and regulate the output:

\\[ \boxed{9\text{–}20\text{ V input}\rightarrow12\text{ V output}} \\]

It must be a true buck-boost converter, not a simple buck or boost converter.

You have seen 300 W-labelled modules on Amazon. They could be candidates, but we should check:

- Actual continuous output power.
- Continuous output current at 12 V.
- Input voltage range, including 9 V.
- Efficiency at the intended operating point.
- Cooling requirements.
- Thermal derating.
- Input and output terminal ratings.
- Overcurrent and thermal protection.

A 300 W advertisement alone does not prove that the module can continuously deliver the required power in our enclosure.

# **7. Battery charging – the remaining major challenge**

This is the component we have not yet satisfactorily sourced.

## **7.1 What we need**

The charger must accept the 20 V output from the PD trigger and charge the 3S battery.

The required behaviour is:

\\[ 20\text{ V DC input} \rightarrow \text{3S Li-ion CC/CV charger} \rightarrow 12.6\text{ V battery} \\]

The charger should have:

- Input voltage compatible with 20 V DC.
- 12.6 V final charging voltage.
- Adjustable charging current.
- Constant-current and constant-voltage operation.
- Proper charging termination.
- Appropriate protection and thermal management.
- Compatibility with the battery's BMS and connection arrangement.

## **7.2 Why the commonly available boards are unsuitable**

We found several products advertised as 3S 12.6 V chargers, but their input specifications do not match our requirement.

| Charger type found                | Problem                                                          |
| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| USB-C 3S charger boards           | Many accept only 5 V input                                       |
| Small Type-C boost charger boards | Designed to boost 5 V to 12.6 V                                  |
| Robu 12.6 V AC charger            | Uses AC mains, not 20 V DC                                       |
| Generic CC/CV DC-DC modules       | May not provide complete Li-ion charging termination and control |

A simple adjustable buck converter set to 12.6 V is not a proper battery charger. Likewise, the BMS does not make an unsuitable power supply safe to use as a charger.

