- A balance charger needs access to the individual group voltages.
- The main positive and negative wires alone do not provide individual cell-group voltage measurements to a conventional balance charger.

The battery supplier needs to confirm the pack wiring and whether a balance connector is available.

# **8. Power calculations**

## **8.1 Cooler power consumption**

The Peltier module alone consumes:

\\[ 12\times6=72\text{ W} \\]

The fans and ESP32 add additional power consumption.

The estimated complete system load is approximately 80–85 W.

This is an estimate, not a measured result.

## **8.2 Can the 90 W PD adapter run everything?**

The converter will have some losses.

Assuming 90% efficiency:

\\[ P\_{\text{input}}=\frac{P\_{\text{output}}}{0.9} \\]

For an 85 W output:

\\[ P\_{\text{input}}=\frac{85}{0.9} \\]

\\[ \boxed{94.4\text{ W}} \\]

That exceeds the adapter's 90 W maximum.

Therefore, the PD adapter cannot guarantee full 85 W cooler operation continuously through the converter at 90% efficiency.

The actual result depends on the Peltier power, fan power and converter efficiency.

## **8.3 Our power-management decision**

We agreed that cooling should receive priority.

Battery charging can be reduced or stopped when the Peltier load is high.

The intended approach is:

- Prioritize the cooler.
- Use available remaining input power for battery charging.
- Reduce or stop charging when cooler demand rises.
- Avoid exceeding the PD adapter's negotiated power limit.

The initial target discussed for external power consumption was approximately 75–80 W, subject to actual measurements.

A charging controller or power-management control strategy will be needed to implement this priority reliably. The proposed relay alone does not automatically regulate the battery charging current according to cooler demand.

## **8.4 Battery current**

At the lower end of the battery's operating voltage, the current required to produce 85 W at 90% efficiency is:

\\[ I=\frac{P}{V\eta} \\]

At 9 V:

\\[ I=\frac{85}{9\times0.9} \\]

\\[ \boxed{I\approx10.5\text{ A}} \\]

So the battery and its BMS must be capable of supplying this current continuously, with suitable margin.

The actual permissible current depends on the cells, BMS, wiring, connectors and operating temperature.

## **8.5 Estimated runtime**

The nominal energy is approximately 111 Wh.

At an 85 W load:

\\[ t=\frac{111}{85} \\]

\\[ t\approx1.31\text{ hours} \\]

This is an ideal energy calculation.

After accounting for converter losses, usable battery capacity, voltage cutoff, temperature and operating conditions, the practical runtime may be closer to approximately 0.9–1.1 hours.

This is only a preliminary estimate. Actual runtime must be measured.

